      <h2 id="jobs">The five jobs club management software does</h2>

      <p>When a committee searches for badminton club management software, it usually means one of these jobs, and it is worth deciding which one before reading a single product page. Each is a different problem, and a product's own site will usually tell you which one it was built around.</p>

      <h3>1. Membership</h3>
      <p>A register of who belongs to the club: contact details, membership category, when they joined, when they are due to renew, and the consent and emergency-contact records a committee is expected to keep. Good membership software lets people join through a form, renews them on a date, and tells the secretary who has lapsed.</p>

      <h3>2. Money</h3>
      <p>Subs and session fees. This is the job most clubs are actually shopping for when they say "club management", because chasing payments is what eats a treasurer's week. The software either <strong>collects</strong> the money - card or Direct Debit, through a payment provider - or <strong>records</strong> money collected some other way. Those are different products. A collecting product's real cost over a year is three things added together: any subscription, times twelve if it is monthly; its percentage fee times the money you collect through it; and any fixed fee per payment times the number of payments your members make. The monthly figure on a pricing page is only the first of those, and billing members monthly rather than annually multiplies the third. Our guide to <a href="/guides/collecting-club-subs">collecting club subs</a> works that out, and finds that how often you bill matters more than which method you use.</p>

      <h3>3. Courts</h3>
      <p>Who can play on which court, when. For a club that owns or runs its own courts, this is booking software: members reserve a slot, the system takes payment and may switch on the lights. For a club that hires a block of courts at a leisure centre or school, it mostly is not the club's problem at all - see <a href="#courts">the next section</a>.</p>

      <h3>4. Competitions and leagues</h3>
      <p>Internal tournaments, box leagues and ladders, and for clubs with league sides, the weekly work of finding out who is available and picking the team. The league itself - fixtures, results and the table - is normally run by the league's own organisers on their own site, and club software that touches it is doing the club's side of the job, not the league's.</p>

      <h3>5. The club night</h3>
      <p>Twenty people, four courts, and a court coming free every few minutes. Who plays next, with whom, and against whom, so the games are close and nobody sits out three times in a row. This is the job a peg board does, and it is the one a membership or booking platform is least likely to be built around, because it happens on a hall floor rather than in an office. Software built for it is usually called club-night software; we scored 32 of those products in <a href="/compare">a published comparison</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="courts">Court management software, and why a club rarely buys it</h2>

      <p>Search for badminton court management software and most of what comes back is built for <strong>venues</strong>: sports centres, racquet clubs with their own buildings, and operators selling court time to the public. It handles online booking, payment at the point of booking, membership discounts, and often door access and lighting.</p>

      <p>That is the right tool if your club owns or manages its courts. Most badminton clubs do not. They hire a block of courts on a fixed evening from a leisure centre, school or community hall, and that booking lives in the venue's system, on the venue's terms. Buying court booking software of your own would not change it. What a hiring club actually manages is two things the venue's system does not: what the block costs and how well it is used - <a href="/guides/badminton-court-hire">what clubs pay for court hire, and what is negotiable</a> covers the first - and how the courts are shared out on the night, which is the club-night job above.</p>

      <p>So before comparing court management systems, check which of these you are. If you hire, the court question is really a money question and a club-night question.</p>

      <h2 id="two-tools">One tool or two?</h2>

      <p>All-in-one platforms exist, and for some clubs one is the right answer. The usual trade-off is that a product built around membership, payments and bookings is designed for the office, and the club night on a hall floor gets the least attention. A product built for the club night is the reverse. Neither shape is wrong. It is a question of which job costs your club the most.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>If the treasurer's chasing is the problem,</strong> start with a product that collects money, and judge it on its transaction rate and how it handles the member who never pays.</li>
        <li><strong>If the club night is the problem</strong> - the same four playing together, the late arrival who never gets on, the organiser who never gets a game - start with club-night software, and keep the subs where they are.</li>
        <li><strong>If you run your own courts,</strong> booking software is the core, and everything else hangs off it.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Running one product for membership and money and another for the night works, as long as the two do not each hold a different list of who your members are. Ask both how they import and export a member list before you start.</p>

