      <h2 id="epegboard">Where ePegboard fits, and where it does not</h2>

      <p>ePegboard is club-night software. It is free for clubs. Every capability below links to the page in our customer documentation that describes it, so you can check it rather than take our word.</p>

      <p><strong>What it does:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Runs the club night</strong> - picks the next game when a court frees up, balanced on ratings and weighted for how long people have waited (<a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/club-nights/the-picker/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the picker</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>Keeps the member list the night runs from</strong>, with a join code so players can connect themselves and a membership type of Full, Trial or Guest that sets each person's session cost (<a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/club-nights/members/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">members</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>Records attendance and session payments</strong>, and reports what was expected, paid and still owed across sessions (<a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/club-nights/payments/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">payments</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>Runs handicap doubles competitions</strong> - balanced pairs, a group stage and a knockout (<a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/club-nights/competitions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">competitions</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>Handles a league captain's availability and team selection</strong>, leaving fixtures, results and the table to your league (<a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/league-teams/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">league teams</a>).</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>What it does not do</strong> - and what to look at instead:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Membership administration.</strong> It keeps your roster, but it takes no money, so it cannot sell or renew a membership, and it has no joining form with a fee. For member records with fees attached, look at SportyHQ or ServeLeague; to collect membership fees, ServeLeague, Spond Club or ShuttleOps.</li>
        <li><strong>Collecting subs.</strong> It records what was paid; it does not take card or online payments (<a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/club-nights/payments/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">payments</a>). To collect, look at ShuttleOps, ShuttleStats or Spond Club, or at the plain options in <a href="/guides/collecting-club-subs">collecting club subs</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Court booking.</strong> A session can show a capacity, but that is a signal, not a booking system - there is no reserving or holding a place (<a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/club-nights/scheduling/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scheduling</a>). If you run your own courts, look at SportyHQ, ServeLeague or ClubSpark; if you hire, your bookings stay with the venue.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The attendance, payment-record and reporting side of ePegboard has <a href="/features/club-management">its own page</a>, with screenshots of each report.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask before you choose</h2>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Which job is costing us the most?</strong> Answer this as a committee before anyone opens a pricing page. It decides which category you are shopping in.</li>
        <li><strong>Does it collect money, or record it?</strong> If it collects, add up a year of it: the subscription, the percentage fee on everything you collect, and any fixed fee per payment times the number of payments. That total is the real price, not the monthly figure.</li>
        <li><strong>Does every member need to install an app or create an account?</strong> Compulsory sign-up is where most rollouts stall. Find out what a member who never signs up can and cannot do.</li>
        <li><strong>Can we get our data out?</strong> Export the member list and the payment history in a trial, not after a year.</li>
        <li><strong>Who owns the courts?</strong> If it is the venue, court booking software is not the answer to your court problem.</li>
        <li><strong>Does it run the actual evening?</strong> If the night is chaotic, a platform whose club-night feature is a sign-up list will not fix it.</li>
      </ol>

